---
/**
 * Difference.astro — the short version of the argument (D-264).
 *
 * What it is: the one paragraph of positioning the home page owes a visitor,
 * naming the professional tools directly and respectfully, then saying plainly
 * what Apelles assumes instead.
 * What it does NOT do: claim to be better, faster or easier than Resolve or
 * Premiere, or draw a comparison table. The claim is about a starting
 * assumption, which is a fact about how each tool was designed rather than a
 * competitive boast — and the full argument lives on its own page so this one
 * does not have to cram it.
 */
---

<section class="diff section">
  <div class="shell wrap">
    <div class="lead">
      <p class="eyebrow">Why this exists</p>
      <h2>Resolve and Premiere are magnificent. They were built for somebody else.</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="body">
      <p>
        DaVinci Resolve and Premiere Pro were made by editors, for editors. Every timeline, node
        graph and keyframe panel in them assumes you already have the craft &mdash; years of it.
        That assumption is a fair one, and it is exactly why those tools are so good at what they
        do. It is also the reason most people never make the video they had in mind.
      </p>
      <p>
        Apelles starts from the opposite assumption: that you know what you want and not how to
        build it, and that building it is the machine&rsquo;s job. You describe the result. An agent
        does the cutting, the colour and the graphics &mdash; through the same controls you would
        have used yourself, on the same undo stack, so you can always take one step back and do it
        your own way.
      </p>
      <p class="more">
        <a class="onward" href="/who-its-for/">
          The longer version <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
        </a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  /* A basalt section (D-272): the tonal inverse of the page, not a second dark
     theme — same two neutrals, ochre standing in for terracotta as the working
     link colour, since terracotta's contrast on basalt is too low to read. */
  .diff {
    background: var(--pigment-basalt);
    border-block: 1px solid var(--edge-inverse);
  }

  .wrap {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: clamp(2rem, 1rem + 5vw, 5.5rem);
    align-items: start;
  }

  .eyebrow {
    color: var(--ink-inverse-soft);
  }

  h2 {
    font-size: var(--step-3);
    margin-top: 1rem;
    max-width: 17ch;
    font-weight: 300;
    color: var(--ink-inverse);
  }

  .body p + p {
    margin-top: 1.25rem;
  }

  .body p {
    max-width: 54ch;
    color: var(--ink-inverse-soft);
  }

  .more {
    margin-top: 2rem;
  }

  .onward {
    color: var(--pigment-ochre);
  }

  @media (max-width: 880px) {
    .wrap {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    }
    h2 {
      max-width: 22ch;
    }
  }
</style>
